This file is a short, step-by-step model of a classic analog AM radio 
receiver, listing each stage with the signal equation and key 
relationships so you can follow how the modulated audio m(t) is 
transmitted, converted to an intermediate frequency, detected, and 
amplified to audio.

Detailed points (stage-by-stage)

1. Transmitted AM Radio Signal (antenna receives)
A carrier cos at fc, amplitude modulated by m(t).

   s_RF(t) = A_c [1 + m(t)] cos(2 π f_c t)

2. RF Amplifier
Simple gain applied to the received RF.

   s_RF,amp(t) = G_RF * s_RF(t)

3. Local Oscillator
Provides the mixing tone.

   s_LO(t) = A_LO cos(2 π f_LO t)

4. Mixer (multiplication)
   s_mix(t) = s_RF,amp(t) * s_LO(t)

   Expanded (show sum and difference terms):
   s_mix(t) = (G_RF A_c A_LO / 2) [1 + m(t)] *
              [ cos(2 π (f_c + f_LO) t) + cos(2 π (f_c - f_LO) t) ]

   Intermediate frequency:
   f_IF = |f_c - f_LO|

5. IF Amplifier + Band-pass Filter
BPF selects the desired difference (IF) component and amplifies it.

   s_IF(t) = G_IF A_IF [1 + m(t)] cos(2 π f_IF t)

6. Detector (Envelope Detector)
Final gain applied to the recovered audio.

   e(t) = |G_IF A_IF [1 + m(t)]|

   After DC blocking:
   s_audio(t)  ∝  m(t)

7. Audio Amplifier
   s_out(t) = G_audio * s_audio(t)

Notes / assumptions implied by the file

Idealized components (linear gains, perfect LO, ideal band-pass filtering, 
simple envelope detection).

No explicit treatment of noise, nonlinearity of mixers/detectors, or 
demodulation details beyond envelope detection.
